#{[17592186060050 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 14\\pm 0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060051 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=8\\,\\text{oz},\\,n=100,\\,\\sigma =2\\,\\text{oz}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 8\\pm 0.392\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060053 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=180\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{180}} \\\\ -5\\pm 0.384\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060054 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=100\\,\\text{g},\\,n=10,\\,\\sigma =12\\,\\text{g}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ 100\\pm 9.775\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186089550 :answer/value "50\\%"] [17592186172323 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=80$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.75,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=80\\cdot 0.7=56>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=80\\cdot (1-0.7)=24>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.7\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{80}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.084\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172324 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=100$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=65\\%=0.65,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=100\\cdot 0.65=65>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=100\\cdot (1-0.65)=35>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\n![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.65\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.65(1-0.65)}{100}} \\\\  & 0.65\\pm 0.093\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172325 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of the students at a particular university,\n\n- some of the students select at least one math course, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=150,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who select at least one math course can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{24}{150}=0.16\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=150\\cdot 0.16=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=150\\cdot (1-0.16)=126>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.16\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.16(1-0.16)}{150}} \\\\  & 0.16\\pm 0.059\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172337 :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=80.6+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=80.6+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>4.303)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.025}=4.303.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(3-6)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(7-6)^{2}+(9-6)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=4.303\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.35}{20}}≈0.6\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=5\\pm 0.6\n$$"] [17592186172338 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all college students in the US,\n\n- some of the students surveyed love soccer, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who love soccer can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{40}{125}=0.32\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=125\\cdot 0.32=40>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=125\\cdot (1-0.32)=85>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.32\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.32(1-0.32)}{125}} \\\\  & 0.32\\pm 0.107\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172369 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=85-87=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{35}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{23}}≈1.953\n$$\n\n \n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2\\pm 1.953\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-2\\pm 1.953.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are negative:\n\n$$\n(-2-1.953,\\,-2+1.953)=(-3.953,\\,-0.047)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are negative, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186172370 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=42-41=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{125}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{118}}≈1.055\n$$\n\n \n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 1.055\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $1\\pm 1.055.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$\n\n$$\n(1-1.055,\\,1+1.055)=(-0.055,\\,2.055)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186172372 :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-4-0.1x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=4-0.1x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.1.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(24-28)^{2}+(27-28)^{2}+(30-28)^{2}+(31-28)^{2} \\\\  & =16+1+4+9 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.007}{30}}≈0.04\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.1\\pm 0.04\n$$"] [17592186172373 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=22-29=-7\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{57}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{94}}≈2.518\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -7\\pm 2.518\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172374 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=21-25=-4\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{23}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{28}}≈2.771\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -4\\pm 2.771\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188553 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=60,\\,n=64,\\,s=24.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.656)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{63,0.005}=2.656.\n$$\n\n![](images/55/5596a286f89b2eed9fd6340c00e77915f84883f2d57d1b342b762143811537aa.png)\n\nTherefore, the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & \\pm 2.656\\cdot \\frac{24}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 60 & \\pm 7.968\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188554 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where  \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=120\\,\\text{g},\\,n=81,\\,s=18\\,\\text{g}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{80,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{80,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=81-1=80$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/69/69f7cbb0e67e69c2ef95f9033f41ecd37a0ed21de94bcb903af17284aeb01155.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{80,0.005}=2.639:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.640$ |\n| $80$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.639$ |\n| $81$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.373$ | $2.638$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & \\pm 2.639\\cdot \\frac{18}{\\sqrt{81}}\\,\\text{g} \\\\ 120 & \\pm 5.278\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188555 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=42,\\,n=9,\\,s=6.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\n![](images/84/84f060ec1a5e8a8b3db0c0866ebef9db1c242e944717a5abfd28447e0eddb9be.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}42 & \\pm 3.355\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ 42 & \\pm 6.71\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188556 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=50,\\,n=16,\\,s=8.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.753)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.05}=1.753.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 50\\pm 1.753\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & 50\\pm 3.506\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188558 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=252,\\,n=100,\\,s=81.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.984)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{99,0.025}=1.984.\n$$\n\n![](images/5b/5b33840d7c98bbf799bdc58dc90d84752b3fa84c36d4a9b122a20ec9401b6823.png)\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}252 & \\pm 1.984\\cdot \\frac{81}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 252 & \\pm 16.070\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192979 :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\widehat{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=3.5+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=3.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.101)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{18,0.025}=2.101.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.101\\cdot \\sqrt{8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{24})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.5\\pm 2.8\n$$"] [17592186207277 :answer/value "50\\%"] [17592186207278 :answer/value "30\\%"] [17592186207283 :answer/value "40\\%"] [17592186207284 :answer/value "50\\%"] [17592186207333 :answer/value "1, 200\\%"] [17592186207661 :answer/value "Approximately $25\\%$ of all sparrows in the region have wing lengths longer than $8$ centimeters."] [17592186207662 :answer/value "Approximately $25\\%$ of all birds in the region have wing lengths longer than $8$ centimeters."] [17592186211882 :answer/value "At least $88\\%$ of all consumers will dislike this ad."] [17592186211883 :answer/value "Exactly $88\\%$ of all consumers will dislike this ad."] [17592186211885 :answer/value "At least $88\\%$ of all smartphone users will dislike this ad."] [1759218622791